<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piri Reis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1465–<a href="/years/1553">1553</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piri Reis was an Ottoman admiral and cartographer, best known for his world map of 1513, which preserves part of an early map of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and for his book of navigation, the Kitab-ı Bahriye. As admiral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> fleet he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> from the Portuguese in 1552 but failed to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, and on his return he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1553.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piri-reis.jpg" alt="Piri Reis" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karamanli86 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piri_reis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The only surviving sources for his early life are his own maps, in which he names himself Muhiddin Piri, son of Hacı Mehmed. He was likely born between 1465 and 1470 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gelibolu</a> (Gallipoli), a major Ottoman naval base. His uncle Kemal Reis was a notable corsair, a state-sanctioned pirate, and under him Piri earned the rank of reis, equivalent to a captain. With the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Sultan Bayezid II</a> he sailed west with Kemal Reis and other Ottoman corsairs to aid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> against the Catholic Monarchs, and as a young man helped bombard the forces besieging Málaga in 1487.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kemal Reis and Piri Reis were recruited into the Ottoman Navy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a>. Around the turn of the century Piri Reis fought in the Ottoman–Venetian wars, and at the Battle of Zonchio in 1499 he piloted one of 270 Ottoman ships that broke through a Venetian fleet of 150 to enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. In 1501, in a naval battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, he and his uncle captured a Spaniard who said he had sailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> and probably carried one of the explorer's charts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p>Decades later, as grand admiral of the Ottoman fleet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, he left Suez on 29 October 1547 with sixty ships to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, which had been ceded to a local Arab chief seeking Portuguese protection. The citadel fell to him on 26 February 1548, and in January 1549 his fleet captured the sailors of Portuguese ships and burned the vessels. He led successful campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> but was beheaded after his retreat from the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>All his surviving maps are portolan charts, designed for navigation by dead reckoning. Three works survive in some form: fragments of the world maps of 1513 and 1528 in the Topkapı Palace Museum, and copies of the Kitab-ı Bahriye, since his original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are lost. The 1513 map is drawn on gazelle skin, and about a third of it survives. Its notes credit part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to a lost map by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>, which an inscription says came from the Spaniard captured near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. He gave the map as a gift to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1517.</p><p>The Kitab-ı Bahriye, or Book of the Sea, is the most detailed portolan atlas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, with 130 chapters in its first version and 210 in the second, which was presented to Suleiman in 1526. His 1528 world map reflects newer discoveries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. His maps received little appreciation in his lifetime. After the 1929 rediscovery of the 1513 map at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, fringe theories claimed it showed an ice-free Antarctica, but studies show no significant similarity with Antarctica's coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piri Reis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After an Ottoman fleet under <b>Piri Reis</b> sacked the town in 1552, the Portuguese built the Jalali and Mirani forts in the 1580s, which still overlook the harbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-muscat">Portuguese Muscat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gelibolu, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.41,26.67&amp;year=1522" aria-label="Gelibolu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5205 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Piri Reis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5655 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5655 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.41,26.67&amp;year=1522">What was here in 1522? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
